From 250f7d5c1e8d9add9a123be03453c778d12bbe9b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jesse <jessenic@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 24 Nov 2026 00:45:49 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] Memory: GPU views of the title's CPU memory read the CPU view
 (intro video)

A retail game reserves 24 GB of 64 KiB pages twice in a row (VirtualAlloc flags
0x20402000): once PAGE_READWRITE, once PAGE_NOACCESS | PAGE_GRAPHICS_NOACCESS (0x801). It
maps physical pages only into the first (XMemMapPhysicalPages, ~54,000 calls in the first
minute), yet places GPU resources at the same offset in the second: its Bink video planes
are decoded by the CPU into the first view (BinkRegisterFrameBuffers), and the video shader
samples R8 textures at those addresses plus the reservation's size. On the console the
second reservation is a GPU-only view of the first; here it was separate, empty memory, so
the video drew zero luma and chroma: a green screen for ~15 s after the first A press.

kernelx::mirrors pairs such a no-access graphics reservation of 64 KiB pages with the most
recent CPU-readable one of the same size (forgotten when either is released).
title_textures keeps a texture placed in the GPU view, where no physical page is mapped,
in step with the same offset in the CPU view: its bytes are read and its CPU writes watched
there (Entry::src), while its title address (GetGPUVirtualAddress, GPU copies into it)
stays the GPU view's. A page mapped at the address counts before its first use too
(kernelx::physical_page_at asks the away map, lazy pages included), and mapping or
unmapping pages inside a GPU view (gpu_view_map_gen) makes the textures there take their
source again at the next look, uploaded from the new one. Mappings elsewhere do not bump
it, so they cost nothing.

The 4K planes are now uploaded each video frame: 12.4 fps during the video against 17 fps
of green before (RTX 5070 Ti).

Tests: gpu_view_reservations_name_their_cpu_view;
a_lazily_mapped_page_in_a_gpu_view_is_its_own_source (by Kalmarv in the review, extended
with a texture registered before the mapping).

